      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T08:00:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sure.\n\nBTW, there might be another problem with this apic_bus_cycle_ns, not covered by this patch. The apic_bus_cycle_ns is used to calculate the cpuid 0x15 eax/ebx, to  make the guest\u0027s native_calibrate_tsc() passed. But if apic_bus_cycle_ns is larger than 27, the calculation will make eax to be 0, so does the ebx. In this case, the guest\u0027s native_calibrate_tsc() will return 0 to fallback to the original way which makes the original patch implementing cpuid 0x15 useless. Do we need to check this against the boundary of [1, 27) to guarantee the cpuid 0x15 will have non-zero eax/ebx?",
      "parentUuid": "9919b150_525ca010",
      "revId": "d9a8e6be9b1b7167c4422e129aef05ab4e7d6a63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b80c3e55_b3345c5e",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 514,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T18:13:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch. But...\n\n\u003e Do we need to check this against the boundary of [1, 27) to guarantee the cpuid 0x15 will have non-zero eax/ebx?\n\nBut _which_ non-zero value should we choose then?\n\nIt seems this just shows that this solution with emulated cpuid 0x15 is not the best one, and we should probably treat it as a temporary one, and eventually replace it with some PV-based solution?\n\nAnd in the meantime, crosvm doesn\u0027t use KVM_CAP_X86_APIC_BUS_CYCLES_NS (actually AFAIK KVM_CAP_X86_APIC_BUS_CYCLES_NS is only used for TDX, and was introduced specifically for TDX needs), so for our use cases apic_bus_cycle_ns will always have the default value i.e. 1. So we can probably leave this as is?",
      "parentUuid": "936c2ce6_550408bb",
      "revId": "d9a8e6be9b1b7167c4422e129aef05ab4e7d6a63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "937bb2db_6f25b972",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 514,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T18:19:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Although, well, one small thing we might \"fix\": if EAX (denominator) ends up being zero, we might enforce EBX (numerator) to be zero too?",
      "parentUuid": "b80c3e55_b3345c5e",
      "revId": "d9a8e6be9b1b7167c4422e129aef05ab4e7d6a63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a878b0ed_cce0b298",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 514,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T04:58:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e But which non-zero value should we choose then?\n\nWe just validate if apic_bus_cycle_ns is in the range of [1, 27), if not, return error code to the host. So that eax (denominator) is always non-zero (ebx is non-zero value regardless apic_bus_cycle_ns). We don\u0027t need to choose any non-zero value for eax. As long as eax is non-zero, the TSC calculation is correct.\n\n\u003e It seems this just shows that this solution with emulated cpuid 0x15 is not the best one, and we should probably treat it as a temporary one, and eventually replace it with some PV-based solution?\n\nIf we only accept the apic_bus_cycle_ns in the range of [1, 27) for pVM, this solution should be ok? From the other aspects, maybe PV-based solution would be better, but I don\u0027t have a clear picture right now.\n\n\u003e And in the meantime, crosvm doesn\u0027t use KVM_CAP_X86_APIC_BUS_CYCLES_NS (actually AFAIK KVM_CAP_X86_APIC_BUS_CYCLES_NS is only used for TDX, and was introduced specifically for TDX needs), so for our use cases apic_bus_cycle_ns will always have the default value i.e. 1. So we can probably leave this as is?\n\nWithout checking for apic_bus_cycle_ns, a malicious host would make the pVM fallback to use HPET/PIC calculation, which seems doesn\u0027t have security issue as long as the tsc is untrusted. Then we can leave it as is.\n\n\u003e Although, well, one small thing we might \"fix\": if EAX (denominator) ends up being zero, we might enforce EBX (numerator) to be zero too?\n\nFrom the `native_calibrate_tsc` implementation, it will return 0 either denominator or numerator is 0. Seems we don\u0027t need to enforce numerator to be zero? Or any other consideration for this enforcement?",
      "parentUuid": "937bb2db_6f25b972",
      "revId": "d9a8e6be9b1b7167c4422e129aef05ab4e7d6a63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5920a419_a6f4ae79",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 514,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T10:55:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e From the other aspects, maybe PV-based solution would be better, but I don\u0027t have a clear picture right now.\n\nAt least one advantage I see is that it would provide the guest with 100% precise native TSC frequency value, without the division error. (Maybe that would be especially desirable if we provide secure TSC in the future.)\n\nAnd also, yes, without this strange limitation of [1, 27) for apic_bus_cycle_ns.\n\n\u003e Without checking for apic_bus_cycle_ns, a malicious host would make the pVM fallback to use HPET/PIC calculation, which seems doesn\u0027t have security issue as long as the tsc is untrusted. Then we can leave it as is.\n\nYeah, the reason for adding this cpuid 0x15 emulation was to prevent power and performance issues (in cases when pVM fails to calibrate TSC and falls back to using periodic timer interrupts, causing high CPU usage), not pKVM/pVM security issues.\n\nWe could return an error to the host if apic_bus_cycle_ns is \u003e27 as you suggested, just to detect this issue easier, if it ever happens. But I don\u0027t think it will happen in practice (since we\u0027re not really gonna use non-default apic_bus_cycle_ns values), so I\u0027m not sure it\u0027s worth adding extra code for that.\n\n\u003e From the native_calibrate_tsc implementation, it will return 0 either denominator or numerator is 0. Seems we don\u0027t need to enforce numerator to be zero? Or any other consideration for this enforcement?\n\nI just see that the CPUID 0x15 description in the SDM says: \"If EBX[31:0] is 0, the TSC/”core crystal clock” ratio is not enumerated.\" but doesn\u0027t say what if EBX is not zero bug EAX is zero. So in such case, the behavior we emulate is architecturally undefined, and semantically means division by zero (denominator is 0)?",
      "parentUuid": "a878b0ed_cce0b298",
      "revId": "d9a8e6be9b1b7167c4422e129aef05ab4e7d6a63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1d91a50c_9c9b1ded",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 514,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-11T02:02:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e But I don\u0027t think it will happen in practice (since we\u0027re not really gonna use non-default apic_bus_cycle_ns values), so I\u0027m not sure it\u0027s worth adding extra code for that.\n\nYou mean it will not happen with a well-behaved host, right? But a malicious host can still do what ever it wants, to trigger this. Actually some security issues can only be triggered by a malicious host rather than a well-behaved host, and we still need to fix these security issues. But this one is not a security issue, so I agree it may not be worth adding extra code for that.\n\n\u003e I just see that the CPUID 0x15 description in the SDM says: \"If EBX[31:0] is 0, the TSC/”core crystal clock” ratio is not enumerated.\" but doesn\u0027t say what if EBX is not zero bug EAX is zero. So in such case, the behavior we emulate is architecturally undefined, and semantically means division by zero (denominator is 0)?\n\nMakes sense. Will enforce ebx as zero if eax is zero.",
      "parentUuid": "5920a419_a6f4ae79",
      "revId": "d9a8e6be9b1b7167c4422e129aef05ab4e7d6a63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
